From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] local: timerfd: make the resume notification deferrable

Supersedes 0205, which did not work. See below.

tick_unfreeze() calls timekeeping_resume() every time the s2idle freeze depth
drops from num_online_cpus(), i.e. on every s2idle entry -- not just on a real
resume. timekeeping_resume() calls timerfd_resume(), which was a bare
schedule_work(). That wakes a kworker; a runnable kworker makes the next
enter_s2idle() return immediately; that unfreezes again and re-queues the work.
Self-sustaining at ~9800 cycles/s on x1e80100, measured by ftrace over the
machine_suspend window, with timerfd_resume_work the only work function running
and tick_freeze/tick_unfreeze landing in the same microsecond.

Why 0205 (gating on inject_sleeptime) failed: on arm64 the current clocksource
is arch_sys_counter, which carries CLOCK_SOURCE_SUSPEND_NONSTOP, so
clocksource_stop_suspend_timing() returns the real elapsed time across the
freeze -- ~100 us, but nonzero. inject_sleeptime is therefore true on every
cycle and the guard never fires. Confirmed on the running kernel: the wakeup
stacks still contain timerfd_resume, which is only reachable through that
branch.

Two other guards are also wrong, recorded so they are not retried:

  - Gating on the mono->real offset changing. It genuinely changes every cycle:
    __timekeeping_inject_sleeptime() calls tk_set_wall_to_mono(), moving
    offs_real by the injected delta.
  - Skipping timerfd_resume() on the s2idle path outright. A long s2idle is a
    real suspend from userspace's point of view, and timerfds registered with
    TFD_TIMER_CANCEL_ON_SET (systemd keeps one to detect clock changes, so
    cancel_list is not empty in practice) must still learn that time jumped.

The property actually wanted is not "do not notify" but "notify without waking
an idle CPU", which is exactly what a deferrable timer provides: per
include/linux/timer.h, TIMER_DEFERRABLE "will not cause a CPU to come out of
idle just to service it; instead, the timer will be serviced when the CPU
eventually wakes up with a subsequent non-deferrable timer."

The delay must be nonzero -- queue_delayed_work_on() short-circuits delay == 0
into __queue_work(), reintroducing the wakeup.

Local patch: derived from this tree, mainline not checked for an existing fix.
Nothing in the mechanism is X1E-specific.

Not-signed-off-by: local build
---
 fs/timerfd.c | 22 +++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

--- a/fs/timerfd.c
+++ b/fs/timerfd.c
@@ -122,15 +122,31 @@
 	timerfd_clock_was_set();
 }
 
-static DECLARE_WORK(timerfd_work, timerfd_resume_work);
+static DECLARE_DEFERRABLE_WORK(timerfd_work, timerfd_resume_work);
 
 /*
  * Invoked from timekeeping_resume(). Defer the actual update to work so
  * timerfd_clock_was_set() runs in task context.
+ *
+ * The work is deferrable, and never queued with delay 0, because
+ * tick_unfreeze() reaches timekeeping_resume() on *every* s2idle entry, not
+ * just on a real resume. An immediate schedule_work() there wakes a kworker;
+ * a runnable kworker makes the next enter_s2idle() return at once; that
+ * unfreezes again and re-queues the work. The loop sustains itself and the
+ * system never reaches system sleep.
+ *
+ * A deferrable timer never brings a CPU out of idle -- the notification is
+ * delivered on the next wakeup that was going to happen anyway. That is soon
+ * enough for a best-effort clock-was-set hint which is already deferred to a
+ * workqueue, and it keeps the notification for a genuinely long s2idle, which
+ * simply skipping the call on this path would lose.
+ *
+ * The delay must be nonzero: queue_delayed_work_on() short-circuits delay == 0
+ * into __queue_work(), which is precisely the wakeup being avoided.
  */
 void timerfd_resume(void)
 {
-	schedule_work(&timerfd_work);
+	schedule_delayed_work(&timerfd_work, 1);
 }
 
 static void __timerfd_remove_cancel(struct timerfd_ctx *ctx)
